<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/kitchen-interior.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/kitchen-interior-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/kitchen-interior.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Frozen Pipe &amp; Leak Claims</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Burst Pipe and Water Damage Appraisal in New Hampshire</h1><p class="lede">A January cold snap and a heating system that quits for a day is enough. Water from a burst line spreads fast and hides well, and first estimates often price only what was visible on the day of inspection.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>Peak season</small><b>December – March</b></li><li><small>High risk</small><b>Camps &amp; vacant homes</b></li><li><small>Often missed</small><b>Hidden moisture</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>A cold-weather state’s most predictable claim</h2>
<p>Few things are as reliable as a New Hampshire freeze-up claim. Overnight lows well below zero in Coös and northern Grafton County, a heating oil delivery that came a day late, a power outage after an ice storm — the cause varies, but the result is the same. Water lines in exterior walls, crawlspaces and unheated additions split, and when things thaw, water runs until someone notices.</p>
<p>In occupied homes that might take a few minutes. In a seasonal camp on Squam Lake or a second home in Waterville Valley, it can take weeks.</p>
<h2>Camps, cottages and the heat condition</h2>
<p>Many policies contain a freezing condition: damage from frozen plumbing is covered only if you used reasonable care to maintain heat, or shut off the water supply and drained the system. Policies also often limit coverage when a home has been vacant or unoccupied beyond a set period. These provisions are common in the Lakes Region and White Mountains, where houses sit empty for months.</p>
<ul>
<li>Keep records of heating oil or propane deliveries and service visits.</li>
<li>Note dates you or a caretaker checked the property.</li>
<li>If the camp was winterized, keep the plumber’s invoice.</li>
<li>Save any data from remote temperature sensors or smart thermostats.</li>
</ul>
<p>Disputes about whether these conditions were met are coverage questions, not appraisal questions. If the carrier is relying on one to deny the claim, talk to a New Hampshire attorney.</p>
<h2>Where water estimates leak value</h2>
<h3>Scope that stops at the stain</h3>
<p>Water travels along framing, under flooring and down through ceilings. Drywall that looks dry can be wet at the bottom plate. Hardwood can cup weeks after mitigation ends. We use moisture readings and, where appropriate, small inspection openings to define the real extent of the damage.</p>
<h3>Flooring continuity</h3>
<p>When wide-pine or oak flooring runs uninterrupted through several rooms, replacing one room’s worth rarely produces a reasonable result. The same goes for tile that’s no longer available.</p>
<h3>Mitigation pricing</h3>
<p>Drying equipment, extraction and antimicrobial work are often billed by a mitigation contractor and then questioned by the carrier. We review those invoices against the work actually documented.</p>
<h3>Mold limits</h3>
<p>Many policies cap mold remediation at a sublimit. Knowing where water damage ends and mold coverage begins affects the final number.</p>
<h2>An illustrative comparison</h2>
<p>The figures below are illustrative only, to show how a first estimate can differ from a fuller scope on the same loss.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Illustrative first estimate</th><th>Illustrative full scope</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Kitchen ceiling drywall</td><td>Priced</td><td>Priced, plus insulation above</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flooring</td><td>Kitchen only</td><td>Kitchen, hall and dining room (continuous)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lower cabinets</td><td>Not included</td><td>Replaced; toe-kicks and sides swelled</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electrical</td><td>Not included</td><td>Inspect and replace wet outlets and wiring</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overhead &amp; profit</td><td>Not included</td><td>Included where several trades are needed</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Wells, pumps and the boiler room</h2>
<p>Plenty of New Hampshire homes run on private wells and oil-fired boilers. A frozen well line or a burst boiler zone may involve pump replacement, pressure tanks, and a heating system that needs more than a new pipe. Whether the mechanical repair itself is covered — as opposed to the water damage it caused — depends on the policy, so we separate those items clearly.</p>
<p>If the water came from an ice dam rather than a pipe, our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/ice-dam-roof-snow-appraisal/">ice dam and roof snow appraisal</a> page explains how those claims differ.</p>

<h2>What owners ask about this</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is a slow leak covered the same as a burst pipe?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not always. Many policies exclude damage from continuous or repeated seepage over a long period, while covering sudden and accidental discharge. The facts of how and when the leak began matter.</p></div></details><details><summary>Should I start drying before the adjuster comes?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most policies require you to take reasonable steps to prevent further damage. Calling a mitigation company promptly is usually appropriate; photograph everything first and keep all invoices.</p></div></details><details><summary>My camp was empty for the winter. Am I covered?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on your policy’s freezing, vacancy and unoccupancy provisions and on the steps you took. Read those sections closely and get legal advice if coverage is in doubt.</p></div></details></div>
<h2>Services that often overlap</h2><div class="related"><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/ice-dam-roof-snow-appraisal/"><small>Service</small><span>Ice Dams &amp; Heavy Roof Snow</span><svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/homeowners-claim-appraisal-nh/"><small>Service</small><span>Single-Family Home Claims</span><svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/insurer-estimate-audit/"><small>Service</small><span>Line-by-Line Estimate Audit</span><svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div>
